<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bede</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 673–735</span> · Also known as the Venerable Bede, Saint Bede, Venerable Bede</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bede was a Northumbrian monk and scholar of the twin monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth–Jarrow</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</a>, completed in 731, is the principal source for the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdoms and their conversion, and his works on chronology popularised dating events from the birth of Christ. Known as the Venerable Bede, he was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1899.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bede.jpg" alt="Bede" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: http://www.e-codices.unifr.ch/de/bke/0047/1v · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E-codices_bke-0047_001v_medium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
